{
  "id": "evaluate-reverse-polish-notation",
  "questionId": "evaluate-reverse-polish-notation",
  "slug": "evaluate-reverse-polish-notation",
  "title": "Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Stack",
  "patterns": [
    "stack"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "in postfix notation, by the time you reach an operator, both numbers it needs have already appeared - where would you keep them in the meantime?",
    "split tokens on spaces and process left to right: push numbers onto a stack, and when you see an operator, pop the two most recent numbers",
    "watch the order: for '-' and '/', the second-popped number is the left operand and the first-popped is the right operand, since it was pushed later",
    "push the result of each operation back onto the stack; at the end exactly one value remains, the answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "swapping operand order for non-commutative operators (- and /), computing b-a instead of a-b",
    "using language-default division/modulo that floors instead of truncating toward zero for negative results",
    "not parsing multi-digit or negative-number tokens correctly when splitting on spaces"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a space-delimited sequence of tokens representing an\narithmetic expression in Reverse Polish Notation (postfix notation). Each\ntoken is either an integer or one of the operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, and\nin RPN an operator always applies to the two numbers immediately before\nit.\n\n**Evaluate the expression and return the result.**\n\nDivision between two integers truncates toward zero, not floor division.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= number of tokens <= 10^4",
      "each token is an integer in [-200, 200] or one of the operators +, -, *, /",
      "the answer and all intermediate results fit in a 32-bit signed integer",
      "division truncates toward zero, not floor division"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "division that would floor differently than it truncates (e.g. negative results)",
      "a single integer token with no operators",
      "an expression that produces a negative final result"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tokens = \"2 1 + 3 *\"",
        "output": "9",
        "expectedOutput": "9",
        "explanation": "2 1 + evaluates to 3, then 3 3 * evaluates to 9"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tokens = \"4 13 5 / +\"",
        "output": "6",
        "expectedOutput": "6",
        "explanation": "13 5 / truncates to 2, then 4 2 + evaluates to 6"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tokens = \"10 6 9 3 + -11 * / * 17 + 5 +\"",
        "output": "22",
        "expectedOutput": "22",
        "explanation": "9 3 + = 12; -11 * 12 = -132; 6 -132 / truncates to 0; 10 0 * = 0; 0 17 + = 17; 17 5 + = 22"
      }
    ],
